  "raw": {
    "https://reed-college.breezy.hr/p/8fe1fc27a476-postdoctoral-researcher-biochemistry": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher, Biochemistry",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Researcher, Biochemistry at Reed College %HEADER_COMPANY_WEBSITE% %HEADER_EMPLOYEES% Postdoctoral Researcher, Biochemistry Portland, OR %LABEL_POSITION_TYPE_FULL_TIME% Chemistry %BREADCRUMB_JOB_OPENINGS% Postdoctoral Researcher, Biochemistry %BUTTON_APPLY_TO_POSITION% Applications are invited for a postdoctoral researcher in Structural Biochemistry Research at Reed College. This is a full-time, one-year position with the possibility of renewal based on performance and the availability of funding. The successful candidate will be closely mentored by Prof. Shivani Ahuja, and will have the opportunity to contribute to and expand existing project(s) as well as mentor undergraduate students. Ahuja lab focuses on elucidating the molecular structure and mechanisms underlying biological processes using cutting-edge structural biology techniques. The role balances hands-on experimental work with critical data analysis and contributes significantly to the research group's overall scientific output. The Ahuja Lab has a well-equipped wet-lab space and office space with workstations. Successful candidates will have a chance to showcase their research at both local and national conferences. Salary for this position starts at $63,000-$67,000 aligned with the National Institute of Health postdoctoral salary scale, which can be found at: https://grants.nih.gov/grants/guide/notice-files/NOT-OD-26-044.html . Pay offers are contingent upon experience and are determined through an internal pay assessment. This is an exempt (overtime eligible) position in grade PD. Reed College offers an exceptional benefits package, including comprehensive medical and dental insurance, a 403(b) retirement plan with 10% employer contribution after one year of service, 22 days of paid vacation, paid holidays, and campus amenities such as membership to the campus fitness center and discounted Trimet transit passes. This is a full-time (part-time) role with typical work hours of 8:30 a.m. to 5:00 p.m. with a one-hour unpaid lunch. Reed College prohibits unlawful discrimination on the basis of race, color, national origin, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, age, marital or familial status, military status, veteran status, genetic information, physical or mental disability, pregnancy, or any other category protected by federal, state, or local laws that apply to the college, in any area, activity or operation of the college, including in its employment policies, educational policies, admission policies, scholarship and loan programs, housing policies, athletic programs, and other school-administered programs. Who you are: You have hands-on experience with protein expression, purification, and characterization techniques. For example, size exclusion chromatography You have hands-on experience with structural biology methods such as X-ray crystallography and/or cryo-electron microscopy is highly desirable You enjoy mentoring undergraduates from a variety of individual backgrounds You are enthusiastic about learning and contributing to scientific advancements You have strong communication and collaboration skills You can work independently and take ownership of projects You are an effective scientific communicator You are committed to research integrity and laboratory safety You are resourceful and adaptable You are proactive about professional growth and scholarly contribution What you’ll do: Perform protein expression, purification, and crystallization Conduct Cryo-EM and X-ray crystallography experiments and analyze structural data Assist with biochemical and biophysical characterization of protein-ligand interactions Contribute to the design and execution of experiments Mentor undergraduate students working on research projects, guiding their learning in experimental design, data analysis, and lab protocols. Generate and keep track of goals, hypotheses, experimental and data analysis protocols, results, data, conclusions, plans, and progress on projects. Keep track of published and unpublished precedents and contribute original ideas. Maintain lab equipment, cleanliness, and safety protocols. Stay current with relevant scientific literature and contribute to project discussions Contribute to lab maintenance, including managing lab equipment and supplies Collaborate on or lead grant proposals to secure funding for new and ongoing research projects. Assist in drafting progress reports for grants and other funding sources. Collaborate effectively with team members and contribute to a positive work environment Co-author manuscripts for publication in peer-reviewed journals. Participate in presenting research findings at conferences. Qualifications: Ph.D. in chemistry or related field or ABD status Demonstrated experience with biochemical and structural biology research methods Applications: A cover letter , curriculum vitae , and contact information for 2 references are required. Evaluation of applications will begin immediately. Applications will be accepted until the position is filled. For more information about this position, please contact Prof. Shivani Ahuja. Reed College replies to every candidate. We will confirm that your application has been received, and if you are not selected, we will let you know as soon as possible. %BUTTON_APPLY_TO_POSITION% %FOOTER_POWERED_BY% breezy"
    },
    "https://reed-college.breezy.hr/p/acf1da28c5b8-data-and-systems-analyst-admission-operations": {
      "title": "Data and Systems Analyst, Admission Operations",
      "description": "Data and Systems Analyst, Admission Operations at Reed College Company Website Employees Data and Systems Analyst, Admission Operations Portland, OR Full-Time Admission and Financial Aid $66,000 – $70,000 / Annually Job Openings Data and Systems Analyst, Admission Operations Apply To Position As part of the Admission Operations Team, this position serves as the technical administrator for the Slate Customer Relationship Management (CRM) system. The role configures, maintains, and optimizes Slate to support efficient admission and financial aid processes, data integrity, reporting, and an effective experience for prospective students and applicants. This position reports to the Senior Associate Dean of Admission Operations, and is under the division of the Vice President and Dean of Admission and Financial Aid. This is not a remote position. Reed College offers an exceptional benefits package, including comprehensive medical and dental insurance options for you and your dependents, 403(b) retirement plan with 10% employer contribution (after one year of service), educational assistance for employees and their children, 22 days of paid vacation, paid holidays, half-day Fridays in the summer, and many other campus amenities. This is a full-time role with typical work hours of 8:30 a.m. to 5:00 p.m. with a one-hour unpaid lunch. The starting salary for this position is $66k-$70k. Pay offers are contingent upon experience and are determined through an internal pay assessment. This is an exempt position in grade 6. Who You Are You approach work with accuracy, thoroughness and careful attention to details. You communicate clearly, consistently and appropriately. You are adaptable and nimble. You adjust as priorities, needs and circumstances change. You take initiative, identify what needs to be done and move forward independently. You are comfortable with ambiguity and can navigate situations where the path forward is not fully defined. You are documentation minded and are able to create and update documentation that supports consistency, knowledge sharing and continuity. You are an active listener. You seek to understand others’ needs and perspectives before identifying solutions or taking action. You are process oriented and are able to identify opportunities for improvement and consider the impact of changes. You build positive working relationships, share information and contribute effectively toward common goals. You understand how process, people, data  and technology connect and consider the broader impact of decisions and changes. What You’ll Do Configure and maintain Slate forms, portals, reader workflows, application components, queries, rules, and communications that support recruitment, application processing, event management, and enrollment operations. Design, build, and support Slate-based solutions using configuration tools and supported technologies. Manage and support the full application operations lifecycle, including application setup, data imports, processing workflows, integrity checks, deferrals, deposits, extension requests, and ongoing operational maintenance. Monitor, troubleshoot, and maintain inbound and outbound data exchanges between Slate, Banner, third-party application systems, external vendors, and institutional data systems to ensure data accuracy, completeness, and timeliness. Develop and maintain integrations, import/export processes, transformation logic, validation rules, and system workflows that support efficient and reliable applicant data flow across systems. Ensure data integrity, security, permissions, and compliance through regular audits, quality control processes, and proactive system monitoring. Build and maintain queries, population selections, reports, and operational dashboards that support recruitment strategy, application review, historical analysis, and data-informed decision making Partner with admissions, financial aid, and campus stakeholders to identify operational challenges, streamline workflows, improve processes, and implement scalable technical solutions. Provide advanced technical support and troubleshooting for Slate users, application processing issues, integrations, and system-related operational concerns Monitor system performance, logs, and communications to identify issues, resolve errors, and support continuous system improvement and operational efficiency Create and maintain clear documentation for workflows, configurations, integrations, business processes, and system dependencies to ensure operational continuity and cross-functional support Provide flexible support for a wide range of logistical, clerical, and office coordination needs to ensure smooth daily operations and assist the Admission operations team as priorities evolve. Minimum Qualifications Bachelor's degree or experience in lieu 3 years experience working with Customer Relationship Management (CRM) systems Apply To Position Powered by breezy"
    },
    "https://reed-college.breezy.hr/p/51f880c30336-director-of-major-and-planned-gifts": {
      "title": "Director of Major and Planned Gifts",
      "description": "Director of Major and Planned Gifts at Reed College %HEADER_COMPANY_WEBSITE% %HEADER_EMPLOYEES% Director of Major and Planned Gifts Portland, OR %LABEL_POSITION_TYPE_FULL_TIME% Advancement $165,000 – $172,000 / Annually %BREADCRUMB_JOB_OPENINGS% Director of Major and Planned Gifts %BUTTON_APPLY_TO_POSITION% The Director of Gift Planning and Major Gifts is a senior leader within Reed College’s Advancement office, responsible for advancing the college’s philanthropic priorities through strategic leadership of major gift and gift planning programs. The Director will oversee a program focused on securing philanthropic support while strengthening Reed’s long-term culture of philanthropy. The Director will lead and manage a team of development professionals spanning major gifts ($100,000 plus) and planned gifts, providing coaching, strategic direction, and accountability to ensure strong portfolio management, collaborative fundraising practices, and successful donor outcomes. The role combines team leadership with frontline fundraising, including management of a personal portfolio of leadership-level donors capable of making six- and seven-figure commitments through current and deferred gifts. This position reports to the Executive Director of Development Leadership and Strategies, under the division of the VP of College Advancement, and primarily works on campus interfacing with faculty and staff. Reed College offers an exceptional benefits package, including a comprehensive medical and dental insurance option for you and family, 403(b) retirement plan with 10% employer contribution (after one year of service), educational assistance for employees and their children, 22 days of paid vacation, paid holidays, half-day Fridays in the summer, and many other campus amenities. This is a full-time role with typical work hours of 8:30 a.m. to 5:00 p.m. with a one hour unpaid lunch. The starting salary for this position is $165,000-172,000. Pay offers are contingent upon experience and are determined through an internal pay assessment. This is an exempt position in grade 12. Who You Are You can design and execute long-term, integrated strategies that align major gifts and planned giving with institutional priorities and campaign goals You have the strong ability to lead, coach, and develop fundraisers, setting clear expectations, inspiring bold thinking, improving performance, and building a collaborative, high-functioning, and accountable team You have demonstrated success securing six- and seven-figure gifts You have expertise in managing donor portfolios (50+ prospects), prioritizing moves management activity, and maintaining a healthy pipeline using data-driven decision-making You are comfortable using metrics, reporting tools, and performance data to guide strategy and assess progress You possess exceptional interpersonal skills to build trust with high-net-worth individuals, alumni, faculty, and institutional leaders You have the ability to work seamlessly across Advancement functions to create a cohesive donor experience You have the capacity to articulate the institution’s mission and funding priorities in a compelling, donor-centered way You have strong project management skills to balance leadership responsibilities with an active fundraising portfolio and campaign initiatives You possess genuine alignment with Reed College’s values and the ability to foster a culture of philanthropy that is inclusive, collaborative, and mission-driven What You’ll Do Strategic Leadership Develop and implement an integrated strategy for major gifts and gift planning that aligns with Reed College’s fundraising priorities and campaign goals Lead coordination between major gifts and gift planning to ensure cohesive donor strategies that maximize both current and deferred philanthropic support Contribute to campaign planning Team Leadership and Management Supervise a team of fundraisers, including coaching and performance management Establish clear goals, metrics, and accountability systems aligned with institutional fundraising targets Support training initiatives for advancement staff related to major gifts and gift planning concepts Foster a collaborative, inclusive, and mission-driven team culture Manage and actively cultivate a portfolio of 50+ leadership-level donors Pipeline and Portfolio Management Help shape fundraising policies, procedures, and best practices Partner with Advancement Operations to develop reporting tools, analytics, and performance metrics Collaborate with prospect researcher to maintain a strong pipeline prospects Collaboration and Stewardship Partner with administrators and faculty to advance donor relationships and solicitations Work closely with Annual Giving, Donor Relations, Alumni Relations and Parent Engagement, to support donor experience Strengthen stewardship practices that promote long-term engagement Minimum Qualifications 5 years experience managing fundraising staff 10 years of fundraising experience Bachelor’s degree or experience in lieu Preferred Qualifications Demonstrated success securing major gifts and planned gifts, including six-and seven-figure commitments Application Instructions A resume is required. You can submit this document in any format, but pdf works best. Applications will be considered as they are submitted. You are encouraged to apply before September 18. Reed College replies to every candidate. We will confirm that your application is received, and if you are not selected, we will let you know as soon as possible. %BUTTON_APPLY_TO_POSITION% %FOOTER_POWERED_BY% breezy"
    },
    "https://reed-college.breezy.hr/p/1bc4f4a9a498-staff-counselor": {
      "title": "Staff Counselor",
      "description": "Staff Counselor at Reed College Company Website Employees Staff Counselor Portland, OR Full-Time Health & Counseling Services $65,000 – $67,000 / Annually Job Openings Staff Counselor Apply To Position The Staff Counselor is a member of the Health & Counseling Center (HCC) team who focuses on providing services to support the success and mental well-being of students. Clinical services will include conducting initial assessments, providing individual and group therapy workshops, consultation, outreach, programming, and collaboration with campus partners. This position reports to the Director of Health and Counseling Center. This is a full-time benefits-eligible 10-month position, working August through May. Benefit eligibility will continue during the summer recess. Hours for this position are Monday through Friday, 8:30 a.m. to 5:00 p.m with occasional weekends and after hours for on-call rotation. Reed College offers an exceptional benefits package, including comprehensive medical and dental options, 403(b) retirement plan with 10% employer contribution (after one year of service), paid vacation, paid holidays and many other campus amenities. The starting salary range for this position is $77,000–$80,000 annually for a 12-month assignment. This position is a 10-month assignment, working August through May; therefore, the annual salary will be prorated to approximately $65,000–$67,000 to reflect the non-working months. Pay offers are dependent on experience and are determined in conjunction with an internal pay equity assessment. This is an exempt position in grade 7. Who You Are You are compassionate, kind, and empathic to both clients and colleagues and possess an innate desire to help people You are self-aware and understand your own culture, identity, biases, prejudices, power, privilege, and stereotypes You have strong clinical and crisis management skills You have an understanding of trauma and trauma awareness You understand the developmental challenges of late adolescent aged students You are committed to community engagement, both within the campus community and the larger Portland area to help clients access resources You are passionate about your work and your area of expertise You understand specific issues related to this work, such as marginalization, social inequities, prejudice, discrimination You are committed to social justice and advocating for students You are aware of your audience and adapt your communication style appropriately What You’ll Do Provide a full range of clinical services for students, including triage, initial assessment, individual and group counseling, crisis intervention, diagnosis, and therapeutic support. Monitor student progress and help students develop effective coping, wellness, and resilience strategies to support mental health and academic success. Provide after hours on-call services as part of the counselor on-call rotation Provide mental health prevention and post intervention outreach programming and education to the college community Coordinate referrals between Counseling Services and college or community service providers Collaborate with other professionals on an interdisciplinary team in the Health & Counseling Center Maintain current, accurate, and timely clinical documentation in accordance with state and federal law, professional ethics, and Counseling Services policies Review student records to assess progress, effectiveness, and appropriateness of treatment services provided Attend and participate in department meetings, division meetings, and staff development programs as assigned Provide culturally responsive and identity affirming individual and group counseling Minimum Qualifications Master’s degree in Social Work, Counselor Education, Marriage and Family Therapy or related field Current Oregon license (LCSW, LPC, LMFT) One year of experience providing services to clients, including crisis intervention and support services to high-risk clients Apply To Position Powered by breezy"
    },
    "https://reed-college.breezy.hr/p/fc35350f9037-certified-medical-assistant": {
      "title": "Certified Medical Assistant",
      "description": "Certified Medical Assistant at Reed College %HEADER_COMPANY_WEBSITE% %HEADER_EMPLOYEES% Certified Medical Assistant Portland, OR %LABEL_POSITION_TYPE_FULL_TIME% Health and Counseling $21.50 – $23.50 / hour %BREADCRUMB_JOB_OPENINGS% Certified Medical Assistant %BUTTON_APPLY_TO_POSITION% The Certified Medical Assistant (CMA) provides direct and indirect student-centered care and services to the students attending Reed College. This position provides the clinical expertise to ensure that all students receive high quality, inclusive, and safe care. The CMA retains the interests of gender, culture, race, ethnicity, sexual orientation, disabilities, etc., at the very core of their work with students and other staff. This position reports to the Director of the Health and Counseling Center. Reed College offers an exceptional benefits package, including comprehensive medical and dental insurance options for you and your dependents, 403(b) retirement plan with 10% employer contribution (after one year of service), educational assistance for employees and their children, paid vacation, paid holidays, and many other campus amenities. This is a 10 month (per year) full-time position with typical work hours of 8:30 a.m. to 5:00 p.m. with a one-hour unpaid lunch. This is not a remote position. Starting salary for this position starts between $21.50-$23.50 per hour. Pay offers are contingent upon experience and are determined through an internal pay assessment. This is an overtime eligible position on grade NE-4. Who You Are You enjoy working with college aged students and adolescents and retain interest in gender, culture, race, ethnicity, sexual orientation, disabilities and other identities at the very core of your work with students You are able to interact effectively and compassionately with staff and students of diverse backgrounds You are self-aware, in terms of understanding your own culture, identity, biases, prejudices, power, and privilege and seek continued growth in these areas You show initiative and have a strong work ethic You value and demonstrate teamwork You actively participate in self-evaluation by utilizing guidance and constructive feedback in a professional manner You are effective in situations where priorities frequently change Others would describe you as flexible, adaptable and versatile You have excellent interpersonal and customer service skills You collaborate with many individuals (including parents, families, spouses, partners, significant others, other members of the campus community, and other health care providers) in providing patient care and advocacy, education and continuity of care that is in compliance with HIPAA and FERPA You display a high level of integrity You seek knowledge and skills appropriate to college health care systems and integrate new information and methodologies What You’ll Do Coordinate the back office work of the clinic to assist and support the nurses and other providers. Set up exam rooms, including cleaning and stocking supplies in between visits Perform rooming such as greet patients, take vitals, verify medications, and verify allergies Update electronic health records Prepare students for examinations and perform/review routine screening tests Administer vaccinations and medications, as directed Perform safe phlebotomy, collecting and processing specimens Perform basic medical procedures such as ECG, ear lavage, and other minor procedures as directed Follow safety protocols and promote safety in the clinic Assist with front desk receptions including answering phones and scheduling appointments as needed Minimum qualifications You have a current medical assistant certification (AAMA or CCMA) You have a current Basic Life Support (BLS) Six months of medical assistant experience %BUTTON_APPLY_TO_POSITION% %FOOTER_POWERED_BY% breezy"
    },
    "https://reed-college.breezy.hr/p/c851b3a10021-director-of-health-and-counseling-center": {
      "title": "Director of Health and Counseling Center",
      "description": "Director of Health and Counseling Center at Reed College %HEADER_COMPANY_WEBSITE% %HEADER_EMPLOYEES% Director of Health and Counseling Center Portland, OR %LABEL_POSITION_TYPE_FULL_TIME% Health and Counseling $160,000 – $175,000 / Annually %BREADCRUMB_JOB_OPENINGS% Director of Health and Counseling Center %BUTTON_APPLY_TO_POSITION% The Director oversees daily operations for the Health and Counseling Center’s (HCC) Medical and Counseling Services. In this dual role, the Director combines direct patient care with administrative and clinical leadership. The Director is also responsible for supervising staff, managing healthcare and mental health programs, and ensuring full regulatory compliance. The Director facilitates coordinated care, shared protocols, and aligned service delivery to support a holistic model of student health at the HCC. The Director contributes to HCC strategic planning, maintains a visible campus presence, and collaborates with campus partners to support student health and well-being. The position reports to the Associate Dean of Students for Health and Well-being, and primarily works on campus, interfacing with students, student families, faculty, and staff. Reed College offers an exceptional benefits package, including comprehensive medical and dental insurance options for you and your dependents, 403(b) retirement plan with 10% employer contribution (after one year of service), educational assistance for employees and their children, 22 days of paid vacation, paid holidays, administrative half-day Fridays in the summer, and many other campus amenities. This is a full-time role with typical work hours of 8:30 a.m. to 5:00 p.m. with a one hour unpaid lunch, however, availability for some after-hours, weekend consultation, and crisis response is required. The starting salary range for this position is $160,000–$175,000 . Pay offers are contingent upon experience and are determined through an internal pay assessment. This is an exempt position in grade 12. Who You Are You foster an inclusive and welcoming environment , ensuring all students feel respected, supported, and able to access care You provide exceptional clinical care, while balancing safety, accountability, and compliance with respect for student rights and human dignity You demonstrate sound judgment and decisive leadership and foster accountability, collaboration, and continuous improvement You excel at managing complex operations , including staffing, budgeting, scheduling, workflow optimization, and resource allocation You build strong relationships , serving as a trusted partner to students, families, faculty, staff, and community stakeholders You champion an integrated approach to health and wellness , promoting collaboration between medical, counseling, and student support services You ensure compliance and manage risk , maintaining adherence to healthcare and counseling regulations, accreditation standards, and clinical best practices You lead proactively in emergency preparedness and crisis response , actively contributing to multidisciplinary teams, public health response, and community partnerships You use data, assessment, and continuous improvement practices to enhance service delivery, inform decision-making, and achieve strategic goals You advance a culture of wellness and prevention , supporting health promotion initiatives that strengthen the overall student experience and align with the broader mission of the institution What You’ll Do Provide and direct patient care in accordance with licensure and scope of practice Lead day-to-day clinical operations of Medical and Counseling Services, including staffing, scheduling, workflow, and budgetary oversight and management Supervise, mentor, and evaluate medical, counseling, and operations staff while developing and assessing annual Medical and Counseling Services goals aligned with Health & Counseling Center priorities and operational data Lead HCC strategic planning, program development, and quality improvement efforts Serve as a visible campus partner and liaison to students, faculty, staff, and families through participation in health promotion initiatives and multidisciplinary teams, including Behavioral Intervention, Emergency Response, and CARE Ensure compliance with clinical standards and all applicable federal, state, and regulatory requirements, including oversight of regulations and governing bodies such as HIPAA, FERPA, CDC, CLIA-waived laboratory standards, PPMP, OBOP, OSHA, OMB, and OSBN Establish and enforce clinical policies, protocols, and a drug dispensing formulary to govern the administration of medications by authorized personnel within the HCC Direct and maintain immunization, laboratory, and medication management programs in compliance with regulations Support emergency preparedness and infectious disease response in coordination with campus leadership Collaborate with the Associate Dean of Health and Well-being to design data-informed, proactive community engagement that builds a campus culture of wellness Maintain availability for after-hours and weekend consultation and crisis response Perform other duties as assigned Minimum Qualifications Licensed Nurse Practitioner (NP) with at least 8-10 years of clinical experience, including 3-5 years of supervisory and healthcare administrative experience. OR Licensed physician (MD, DO, or ND) with at least 6-8 years of clinical experience, including 3-5 years of supervisory and healthcare administrative experience. Active, unrestricted license as a Physician or Nurse Practitioner in the State of Oregon Active, valid prescriptive and dispensing authority issued by the applicable Oregon licensing board Current Basic Life Support (BLS) certification Preferred Qualifications Drug Enforcement Agency (DEA) registration or eligibility to obtain National certification as an Advanced Practice Nurse/Nurse Executive Leader or Physician board certification in Family Practice, Internal Medicine, Pediatric/Adolescent Medicine, Emergency Medicine, Gynecology, or other college health appropriate ABMS-recognized medical specialty Experience in integrated health systems or cross-functional student services environments, with demonstrated ability to collaborate effectively across departments in support of coordinated care Experience in mental health services %BUTTON_APPLY_TO_POSITION% %FOOTER_POWERED_BY% breezy"
    },
    "https://reed-college.breezy.hr/p/d1977227c1fa-director-of-prospect-research": {
      "title": "Director of Prospect Research",
      "description": "Director of Prospect Research at Reed College %HEADER_COMPANY_WEBSITE% %HEADER_EMPLOYEES% Director of Prospect Research Portland, OR %LABEL_POSITION_TYPE_FULL_TIME% Advancement $82,000 – $90,000 / Annually %BREADCRUMB_JOB_OPENINGS% Director of Prospect Research %BUTTON_APPLY_TO_POSITION% The Director of Prospect Research provides strategic leadership for prospect development, research, portfolio management, and fundraising analytics. This position partners closely with Advancement leadership and frontline fundraisers to expand the prospective donor pipeline, optimize portfolios, and deliver data-driven insights. This position designs and implements institutional strategies for prospect identification and data utilization to support the college's comprehensive fundraising campaigns and long-term philanthropic growth. This position is responsible for conducting prospect research and creating relevant materials including briefings. Reed College offers an exceptional benefits package, including comprehensive medical and dental insurance options for you and your dependents, 403(b) retirement plan with 10% employer contribution (after one year of service), educational assistance for employees and their children, 22 days of paid vacation, paid holidays, half-day Fridays in the summer, and many other campus amenities. This is a full-time role with typical work hours of 8:30 a.m. to 5:00 p.m. with a one-hour unpaid lunch. The starting salary range for this position is $82,000-90,000. Pay offers are contingent upon experience and are determined through an internal pay assessment. This is an exempt position in grade 8. Who You Are You have experience in prospect research of complex individual, family, foundation, and corporate philanthropy. You are a consistent strategist that is able to identify relationships and connections among prospective donors. You are a collaborative and relational team member and work well with a variety of personality types. You are proactive in identifying opportunities to enhance fundraising strategy and elevate gift officer performance. You are able to produce and translate data to drive initiatives and departmental goals. You are forward looking, innovative, and creative. You are comfortable serving as an advisor to a diverse set of team members, including frontline gift officers and college leadership. You are able to balance strategic leadership with personal goals relating to prospect research conducted and briefings produced. What You’ll Do Prospect Development Strategy & Leadership Develop and lead Reed's comprehensive prospect development strategy to identify, qualify, and advance prospective donors capable of making principal, major, and planned gifts Design and implement proactive prospect identification initiatives that expand the donor pipeline using internal and external data resources Train fundraising staff on prospect management practices, research interpretation, and portfolio management May supervise staff Prospect Research and Portfolio Management Develop and oversee Reed's portfolio management strategy to support fundraiser productivity and donor engagement. Lead annual and ongoing portfolio review processes with frontline fundraisers and leadership, and monitor portfolio health to recommend prospect assignments. Conduct prospect research supporting principal gifts, campaign initiatives, and strategic fundraising efforts Analyze philanthropic capacity, inclination, affinity, and engagement using a variety of public and subscription resources, both on a routine basis and as needed throughout the year Produce prospect profiles, briefing materials, relationship maps, and strategic analyses Data Governance & Best Practices Establish policies and procedures related to prospect management, prospect research, and portfolio management and serve as institutional expert on prospect development methodologies and best practices Ensure compliance with ethical standards established by American Prospect Research Association (APRA) and Council for Advancement and Support of Education (CASE) as well as applicable privacy regulations Minimum Qualifications Bachelor's degree or experience in lieu 5 years of progressively responsible experience in prospect development, prospect research, portfolio management, advancement operations, or fundraising strategy Preferred Qualifications Exceptional written and verbal communication skills Experience developing fundraising dashboards and portfolio analytics Familiarity with APRA and CASE standards and best practices Experience supporting principal and major gift fundraising programs and campaigns within a large, complex organization Demonstrated ability to interpret complex financial and philanthropic information Application Instructions A resume and cover letter are required. You can submit these documents in any format, but pdf works best. Applications will be considered as they are submitted so you are encouraged to apply early. Reed College replies to every candidate. We will confirm that your application is received, and if you are not selected, we will let you know as soon as possible. %BUTTON_APPLY_TO_POSITION% %FOOTER_POWERED_BY% breezy"
    },
    "https://reed-college.breezy.hr/p/c756b7d06360-executive-director-facilities-operations": {
      "title": "Executive Director, Facilities Operations",
      "description": "Executive Director, Facilities Operations at Reed College %HEADER_COMPANY_WEBSITE% %HEADER_EMPLOYEES% Executive Director, Facilities Operations Portland, OR %LABEL_POSITION_TYPE_FULL_TIME% Facilities $175,000 – $195,000 / Annually %BREADCRUMB_JOB_OPENINGS% Executive Director, Facilities Operations %BUTTON_APPLY_TO_POSITION% Reed College is seeking an experienced and collaborative Executive Director of Facilities Operations to provide strategic and operational leadership for the college’s physical campus. Reporting to the Vice President for Finance & Treasurer, this executive-level position oversees facilities maintenance, custodial services, preventive maintenance, grounds, space planning, and capital projects while ensuring a safe, functional, sustainable, and welcoming campus environment. About the Role The Executive Director of Facilities Operations leads both the day-to-day operation and long-term stewardship of Reed College’s facilities. The position develops strategies and policies that support efficient and cost-effective operations, regulatory compliance, effective use of campus resources, and long-term capital needs. This is a highly visible campus leadership role that works closely with senior leadership, faculty, staff, students, the Board of Trustees, contractors, consultants, and community partners. The Executive Director must be able to translate complex facilities, operational, and capital matters into clear recommendations that support institutional priorities. The successful candidate will be a strong operational and people leader who can balance the immediate needs of a complex campus with long-term facilities planning. They will bring sound judgment, strong communication and problem-solving skills, and the ability to build productive relationships across organizational levels while maintaining accountability for projects, budgets, safety, and service. This position is on campus and regularly interfaces with Reed College students, faculty, staff, leadership, and external partners. Benefits and Compensation Reed College offers an exceptional benefits package, including comprehensive medical and dental insurance options for you and your dependents, 403(b) retirement plan with 10% employer contribution (after one year of service), educational assistance for employees and their children, 22 days of paid vacation, paid holidays, half-day Fridays in the summer, and many other campus amenities. This is a full-time role with typical work hours of 8:30 a.m. to 5:00 p.m. with a one-hour unpaid lunch. The starting salary for this position is $ 175,000 - $185,000 per year. For candidates whose experience significantly exceeds the minimum qualifications, the salary may be up to $195,000 . Pay offers are contingent upon experience and are determined through an internal pay assessment. This is an exempt position on exempt grade 13. Who You Are You are a strong, collaborative leader who enjoys developing teams and helping people succeed. You communicate clearly and comfortably with a wide range of audiences. You bring a strong understanding of building trades and technical facilities operations, gained through hands-on experience or active involvement working alongside skilled trades and technical teams. You are experienced in labor relations, employee coaching, and performance management. You are comfortable leading through change and looking for better ways to get work done. You think strategically while staying connected to day-to-day facilities operations. You know how to move capital projects from planning/design and budgeting through successful completion. You understand preventive and deferred maintenance and how to balance immediate needs with long-term priorities. You are comfortable managing budgets, resources, contractors, consultants, and vendors. You understand space planning, infrastructure, and the complexities of maintaining a campus environment. You use data, budgets, timelines, and performance measures to guide decisions and priorities. You thrive in a busy environment and enjoy managing a varied and interesting to-do list. You are adaptable when priorities and plans change, especially when the change leads to a better outcome. You value safety, accountability, collaboration, inclusivity, continuous improvement, and excellent service. What You’ll Do Provide strategic leadership for facilities operations, including maintenance, custodial services, grounds, preventive maintenance, space planning, and capital projects. Develop and implement policies and strategies for facility maintenance and repair, space utilization, design, construction, and long-term campus infrastructure needs. Lead preventive, corrective, and deferred maintenance planning to support the safety, functionality, and long-term sustainability of campus facilities. Plan and manage capital improvement projects through the full project lifecycle, including scope design/development, design, budgeting, execution, and delivery. Direct facilities supervisors and managers and provide leadership to maintenance, custodial, and grounds teams. Develop short- and long-term operational plans, programs, contracts, and priorities for Facilities Services. Manage contractors, subcontractors, professional service consultants, and vendors, including solicitation, selection, negotiation, and performance oversight. Track project and operational performance against budgets, milestones, and other performance indicators and provide reports and recommendations to college leadership. Communicate facilities projects and operational impacts effectively to individuals, departments, buildings, and the broader campus community. Present complex facilities and strategic matters to senior leadership, faculty, the Board of Trustees, and other campus and community groups. Foster a departmental culture centered on safety, accountability, collaboration, inclusivity, service, and continuous improvement. Partners with Human Resources in labor relations and collective bargaining, including union negotiations, contract administration, and implementation of collective bargaining agreements affecting Facilities operations. Performs other duties as assigned Minimum Qualifications 10 years of progressively responsible experience in facilities management, including at least five years in a leadership role. Preferred Qualifications We are particularly interested in candidates who bring experience managing complex facilities operations and who demonstrate: Effective budget management and resource allocation aligned with organizational priorities. Experience leading facilities operations within a higher education environment. Working knowledge of applicable health, safety, and environmental regulations. Experience supporting sustainability and environmentally responsible facilities practices. Knowledge of relevant state, county, and municipal regulations, codes, and reporting requirements. Experience leading represented employees and participating in union negotiations and collective bargaining matters Application Processing Applications will be reviewed as they are received. For priority consideration, candidates are encouraged to apply by November 1, 2026. Reed College replies to every candidate. We will confirm that your application is received, and if you are not selected, we will let you know as soon as possible. %BUTTON_APPLY_TO_POSITION% %FOOTER_POWERED_BY% breezy"
    }
  },
